# RiskGate model threshold alone (experiment 8, "model threshold alone").
#
# One rule: block when the calibrated fraud probability is at least 50%.
# No review band and no other rules, so experiment 8 can compare it with
# rules/default.rules (the same block threshold, plus a review band and
# rules).
#
# Chosen on the VALIDATION month only (month 4: 83,571 payments, 2,850
# fraud) with the backtester's risk_score sweep on the scored table
# data/cache/table_scored_real.rgt. The test month was not looked at.
#
# The choice is by cost, the same accounting experiment 8 uses: a blocked
# legitimate payment loses its amount, and a missed fraud costs its amount
# plus a $15 dispute fee. Blocking pays when the fraud probability p exceeds
# a / (2a + 15), just under 50%, and risk_score is p in percent. On
# validation, scores sit on the calibration's steps (..., 45, 56, 63, ...),
# so any cut from 46 to 56 is the same cut. Every step from 63 up is net
# positive in those terms and every step up to 45 net negative. The step at
# 56 (81 payments, 57% fraud) is -$1,199 because its fraud is small-ticket;
# a cut at 63 would gain that on validation, but 50 is the principled
# break-even and 81 payments are too few to tune to.
#
# Validation: 370 blocked, 71.4% fraud, fraud recall 9.3%, FPR 0.13%,
# fraud-dollar recall 15.0% ($69,189), legitimate dollars blocked 0.36%
# ($38,299). Net of the above accounting, +$34,849.
#
# Not chosen: experiment 1's budget of 1% of legitimate dollars would allow
# a threshold of 35 (661 blocked, 57.2%, fraud-dollar recall 19.0%, 0.67% of
# legitimate dollars), but the extra 291 payments it blocks cost more
# legitimate dollars than they save in fraud (net +$21,231 against
# +$34,849). The isotonic calibration was fitted on this month, so all of
# these validation numbers are optimistic.
#
# Check this file with:  go run ./cmd/rulecheck -lists rules/lists.json rules/model_only.rules
block if :risk_score: >= 50
